{
  "dom_id": "step-e438",
  "name": "Example: Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus",
  "problem": "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm dx} \\int_2^x \\dfrac {t^3} {t-1}\\,\\textrm d t$ for $x>2.$",
  "worked_solution": "The function $\\dfrac {t^3} {t-1}$ is continuous on $[2,x],$ where $x>2.$ So FTC II applies, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{2}^{x}\\frac{{t}^{3}}{t-1}\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
